<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Uwajima Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1614 CE – 1871 CE · capital Uwajima</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Date family domain in southwestern Shikoku whose reforming lord Date Munenari built one of Japan's first steamships.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1614 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uwajima">Uwajima</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyo-province">Iyo</a> province, with 100,000 koku, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-hidemune">Date Hidemune</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a>, who had been passed over as heir to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sendai-domain">Sendai Domain</a>. Hidemune entered Uwajima castle in 1615.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-hidemune">Hidemune</a>'s descendants ruled until 1871 as an independent branch of the Date. The domain lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, fisheries and paper on a rugged coast. In the 1850s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-munenari">Date Munenari</a>, counted among the four wise lords of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, sheltered the Western scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takano-choei">Takano Choei</a>, hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omura-masujiro">Murata Zoroku</a>, later Omura Masujiro, and had a working steamship built with local craftsmen.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-munenari">Munenari</a> took part in the councils that shaped the new government in 1868, and served in early Meiji diplomacy. After abolition in 1871 the domain became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehime-prefecture">Ehime Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/uwajima-domain.jpg" alt="Uwajima Domain" width="320" height="238"><figcaption>Image: PekePON · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uwajima_Castle_and_cherry_blossom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1614 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Uwajima</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Date Hidemune, Date Munenari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Uwajima han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1742&amp;focus=uwajima-domain" aria-label="Uwajima Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8071 972 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Uwajima Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8391 1228L8530 1145M8391 1228L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8391 1228h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8391 1228h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Uwajima. <a href="/atlas?year=1742&amp;focus=uwajima-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
